    22	thread_11_summary:Thread 11 = OPEN_POSITIVE_TARGETS.md §P11 — minimum number of straight lines required to cover all primes <= N under a chosen 2D embedding. Trivially 1 line on Z (primes collinear). Non-trivial in 2D embeddings: (a) Ulam spiral (1963) — diagonal lines correspond to Hardy-Littlewood quadratic-prime sequences f(n) = a*n^2 + b*n + c, dominated by Euler's n^2 + n + 41 and similar; (b) residue-class grid (n_x = n mod p, n_y = n div p), where each row is a prime residue class; (c) polynomial-image grids n -> (P(n), Q(n)) for P, Q polynomial. Quantity: minimum line cover number L(N) of the primes <= N in the chosen embedding. Cross-domain ingredient: Szemeredi-Trotter incidence theorem (1983), Erdos extremal incidence problems, Hardy-Littlewood prime k-tuple conjecture for quadratics. Question: does L(N) admit a polylog or sub-linear computable invariant? Lines of primes correspond to k-tuple-prime polynomial sequences; if covering achieves L(N) ~ pi(N)^alpha for some named alpha < 1, the invariant has algorithmic content for prime-density estimation.
    23	thread_11_a_grade_targets:(a) named-exponent L(N) scaling for one or more 2D embeddings (alpha < 1) with structural reason (HL k-tuple density of quadratic forms, or matched-baseline z-score >= 5sigma showing primes are non-trivially compressible in lines) — first explicit incidence-geometric prime-density theorem in the project; (b) polylog-time approximation of L(N) via line sampling that improves on naive O(pi(N)^2) brute force; (c) closed-form L(N) for a specific embedding (e.g., Ulam spiral) tied to Hardy-Littlewood constants for named quadratic forms.
    24	thread_11_slot_plan:
    25	  slot_1: build Ulam-spiral evaluator. Place primes <= N on Ulam spiral. Compute minimum line cover L(N) via greedy line-detection / LP relaxation at N in {10^4, 10^5, 10^6}. Compare to matched-baseline (random density-pi(N)/N points on the spiral) and HL prediction for the dominant prime-quadratic lines (Euler 41, Heegner 67, etc.).
    26	  slot_2: alternative 2D embeddings — residue-class grid (n mod p, n div p) for p = 2, 3, 5, 7, 11; polynomial-image grid (n, n^2 mod p). Compute L(N) for each. Cross-embedding scaling fit.
    27	  slot_3: theoretical shape — does L(N) follow Szemeredi-Trotter incidence bound L(N) >= pi(N)^{2/3} (random-points lower bound), or beat it via HL alignment? If beats, by what HL-related factor?
    28	  slot_4: algorithmic angle. Is there a polylog-time approximation algorithm for L(N) that exploits HL structure on prime quadratics?
    29	  slot_5: theoretical wrap. Either named-exponent scaling theorem with HL backing, or structural negative documenting why L(N) saturates at random-point Szemeredi-Trotter bound.
    30	slot_5_summary:S488 built p11_hl_singular_series.py and p11_hl_distribution_analysis.py. Computed HL singular series C_f = prod_p (1 - sigma_f(p)/p)/(1 - 1/p) for the 4k^2+bk+c quadratic pairs underlying 377 slope-+/-1 Ulam diagonals at N=10^5 (|c|<=250). Findings: (i) Line-uniform mean HL = 2.022, length-weighted = 2.032, prime-weighted = 2.298 (sample biased high; long central diagonals are HL-rich). (ii) Distribution: 9% of lines HL<1, 23.6% in [1.5,2), 25.7% in [2,2.5), 7.4% with HL>=3. Top-3 prime-rich lines (HL_max ~6.0-6.6) are Heegner-class quadratics. (iii) Naive prediction LP_p/LP_r = 1/avg_HL = 1/2.02 = 0.495 FAILS — empirical c=0.776 much larger. Reasons: 31% of LP weight is on axes (HL=1), LP exploits HL VARIANCE not just mean, sample biased high. (iv) Empirical decomposition: c = 1/(0.31 + 0.69*1.42) = 1/1.29 = 0.776, with <theta>_LP ~ 1.42 = LP-weighted avg HL on LP-active slope-+/-1 lines. (v) Conditional theorem (HL Conj F): LP_p/LP_r -> c < 1 with c determined by LP equilibrium, NOT by closed-form HL identity. c = 0.776 is NOT 1/(2C_2), 6/pi^2, or other named constants. (vi) Closed-form c remains open: requires solving min-cost-flow LP on infinite-Ulam joint-HL bipartite incidence, beyond closed-form analysis. (vii) Trivial bound c >= 1/C_max ~ 1/6.6 ~ 0.15 (using max HL singular series). Empirical c=0.776 well above trivial bound. B grade slot — substantive structural identification + conditional theorem + refutation of naive prediction, but no closed form for c. Thread 11 closes B-grade partial-positive structural overall.
